{
  "title": "{cop:Vorrei} venire, ma {cpa:avrei dovuto} studiare",
  "translation": "I'd like to come, but I should have studied",
  "topics": "The [cop:condizionale presente] and the [cpa:condizionale passato]",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "How to form the [cop:condizionale presente]"
      },
      "If you know the future, you already know the stem. Only the endings change, and irregular verbs keep the same irregular stems: *{fs:sarò} → {cop:sarei}, {fs:avrò} → {cop:avrei}, {fs:verrò} → {cop:verrei}, {fs:andrò} → {cop:andrei}, {fs:farò} → {cop:farei}*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "",
            "parlare → parler-",
            "scrivere → scriver-",
            "dormire → dormir-"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "io",
              "{cop:parler**ei**}",
              "{cop:scriver**ei**}",
              "{cop:dormir**ei**}"
            ],
            [
              "tu",
              "{cop:parler**esti**}",
              "{cop:scriver**esti**}",
              "{cop:dormir**esti**}"
            ],
            [
              "lui / lei",
              "{cop:parler**ebbe**}",
              "{cop:scriver**ebbe**}",
              "{cop:dormir**ebbe**}"
            ],
            [
              "noi",
              "{cop:parler**emmo**}",
              "{cop:scriver**emmo**}",
              "{cop:dormir**emmo**}"
            ],
            [
              "voi",
              "{cop:parler**este**}",
              "{cop:scriver**este**}",
              "{cop:dormir**este**}"
            ],
            [
              "loro",
              "{cop:parler**ebbero**}",
              "{cop:scriver**ebbero**}",
              "{cop:dormir**ebbero**}"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Careful: the future *{fs:parleremo}* (we will speak) has one *m*, and the conditional *{cop:parleremmo}* (we would speak) has two.",
      {
        "h": "What it is for"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Use",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Polite requests",
              "{cop:Vorrei} un caffè. {cop:Potrebbe} aiutarmi?",
              "I'd like… Could you…?"
            ],
            [
              "Wishes",
              "Mi {cop:piacerebbe} viaggiare.",
              "I'd like to travel."
            ],
            [
              "Advice",
              "{cop:Dovresti} riposarti.",
              "You should rest."
            ],
            [
              "Unconfirmed news",
              "Il ministro {cop:sarebbe} a Roma.",
              "The minister is said to be in Rome."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "The [cpa:condizionale passato]"
      },
      "It is formed like the [pp:passato prossimo], with the auxiliary in the conditional: *{cpa:avrei mangiato}, {cpa:sarei partito}*. The same rules for the auxiliary and agreement apply.",
      "It describes what would have happened but did not. It is the tense of regret and missed chances.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Now",
            "Too late"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{cop:Dovrei} studiare. (I should study.)",
              "Avrei dovuto studiare. (I should have studied.)"
            ],
            [
              "{cop:Potrei} venire. (I could come.)",
              "Sarei potuto venire. (I could have come.)"
            ],
            [
              "Vorrei partire. (I'd like to leave.)",
              "Sarei voluto partire. (I would have liked to leave.)"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "With *dovere, potere* and *volere*, the auxiliary is the one the following infinitive would take: *{cpa:Avrei dovuto} studiare*, but *{cpa:Sarei dovuto} partire*."
    ]
  },
